nss_meminfo: map the block table non-cacheable

The meminfo block table is the structure that tells the firmware where
its rings and heap live. The host writes it before the core is released
from reset, and the core reads it through its own view of DDR, so the
two sides only agree if the host's writes have actually reached memory.

Patch 0004 ("rework NSS_CORE_DMA_CACHE_MAINT ops") removed the cache
maintenance around that table without putting anything in its place -
what is left in nss_meminfo_init_block_lists() is a bare NSS_CORE_DSB(),
which orders accesses but does not clean the D-cache. With the table
mapped ioremap_cache(), whatever part of it is still sitting in a dirty
cache line when the core starts is simply not there as far as the core
is concerned.

Mapping the table write-combining removes the dependency on the cache
state entirely, which is what the flush used to guarantee. The table is
touched once at init and never on a hot path, so there is nothing to
lose by giving up cacheability here.

--- a/nss_meminfo.c
+++ b/nss_meminfo.c
@@ -751,7 +751,15 @@
 	}
 
 	map = &mem_ctx->meminfo_map;
-	map->start = (uint32_t *)ioremap_cache(meminfo_start[1], NSS_MEMINFO_MAP_SIZE);
+
+	/*
+	 * Non-cacheable: the core reads this table through its own view of
+	 * DDR before it runs a single instruction of firmware, and patch
+	 * 0004 removed the cache clean that used to make the host's writes
+	 * visible to it. The table is written once at init and never read
+	 * on a hot path, so write-combining costs nothing here.
+	 */
+	map->start = (uint32_t *)ioremap_wc(meminfo_start[1], NSS_MEMINFO_MAP_SIZE);
 	if (!map->start) {
 		nss_info_always("%px: failed to remap meminfo map\n", nss_ctx);
 		return false;
